.TH wmnotch 1 "November 2026"

.SH NAME
wmnotch \- shade the camera notch of the screen

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wmnotch
.RI [ options ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBwmnotch\fR draws a shade over the strip of the screen that is at the height
of the camera notch of a laptop panel. The shade is black at the edge of the
screen and fades away a little below the strip, so that the notch melts into
it. It lies over every window, and what is below it stays visible through it.
While a fullscreen window is shown, the strip is plain black instead.
.PP
It is not meant to be started by hand: \fBwmaker\fR starts it when the screen
has a notch, tells it where the notch is with the \fI_WINDOWMAKER_NOTCH\fR
property of the root window, and stops it when it exits. \fBwmnotch\fR exits
when its standard input is a pipe that gets closed.
.PP
The shade is blended with the contents of the windows that are below it, for
which \fBwmnotch\fR uses the overlay window of the Composite extension. No
compositing manager is needed, and it should not be used together with one.

.SH OPTIONS
The following command line options are available:
.TP
.B \-\-help
print a help message with the list of options.
.TP
.B \-\-version
print the version of Window Maker from which the program comes.

.SH CONFIGURATION
The notch is found from the size of the screen. This can be changed with two
options of the \fIWindowMaker\fR domain of \fBwmaker\fR:
.TP
.B NotchHeight
height of the strip in pixels of the screen, \fIAuto\fR to find it (default),
or \fI0\fR to have no strip.
.TP
.B NotchFade
count of rows below the strip where the shade ends, \fIAuto\fR for 3/4 of the
height of the strip (default).

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wmaker (1)
